    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The Nahda, the “awakening”, was a movement of 19th-century Arab writers, translators and teachers, Muslim and Christian, who sought to renew Arabic culture and join it to modern knowledge.</li><li>Through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printing</a> presses, newspapers, journals, dictionaries, schools and translations, centred in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a>, they created modern Arabic prose and Arabic journalism, drama and the novel.</li><li>Its thinkers debated the reform of religion, government and education and the place of women, and their ideas fed both Islamic modernism and Arab nationalism.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The first play in Arabic, Marun al-Naqqash’s al-Bakhil (The Miser), adapted from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moliere">Molière</a>, was performed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beirut">Beirut</a> in 1847; by 1850 he had built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> near his home for a new play drawn from the Thousand and One Nights.</p></aside>
    </section>

